        <span class="icon">🕕</span>
        <span>6:00 PM – 9:00 PM (Doors 5:45 PM)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-item">
        <span class="icon">🏡</span>
        <span>Birth Sisters of Charlottesville Rock House</span>
      </div>
      <div class="detail-item">
        <span class="icon">✨</span>
        <span>FREE • Community Potluck • Open Seating</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="scroll-indicator">↓</div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT IS IT SECTION -->
<section class="section what-is-it">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2 class="section-title">What Is It?</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">A celebration of village, food, and motherhood</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="content-grid">
    <div class="content-card">
      <h3>Inspired by Ntozake Shange</h3>
      <p>Written in the spirit of "If I Can Cook / You Know God Can" — Shange's vibrant celebration of African diasporic food traditions, the kitchen table as altar, and food as survival, resistance, and joy.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-card">
      <h3>Community as Fifth Voice</h3>
      <p>Four community members read character parts. Everyone else participates in call-and-response throughout (like "for colored girls"). Then we eat together. Then we talk about it.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-card">
      <h3>Part of The Practice</h3>
      <p>This choreopoem is part of The Practice of Living Harmonic Reciprocity — RBSI's community care and action arm focused on joy, celebration, ritual, and reciprocity as core practices.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- BMHW26 THEME ALIGNMENT -->
<section class="section bmhw-theme-section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2 class="section-title">Rooted in Justice & Joy</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Perfectly aligned with Black Maternal Health Week 2026</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="theme-content">
    <div class="theme-pillar">
      <div class="theme-icon">🌱</div>
      <h3>ROOTED</h3>
      <p>In ancestral food traditions passed down through generations. In traditional midwifery wisdom. In Southern doula care. In African diasporic knowledge that kept our mothers alive for centuries. In the deep soil of our grandmothers' kitchens.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="theme-pillar">
      <div class="theme-icon">⚖️</div>
      <h3>JUSTICE</h3>
      <p>Because collective care should be normal, not rare. Because village-centered support is a right, not a privilege. Because this choreopoem challenges who controls knowledge about Black birth and reclaims our stories as our own.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="theme-pillar">
      <div class="theme-icon">✨</div>
      <h3>JOY</h3>
      <p>Not despite the struggle, but alongside it. Celebrating what WORKS when community shows up. Honoring the beauty of healing foods, loving hands, and village in action. This is not crisis — this is triumph.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="theme-statement">
    <p>This choreopoem about African diaspora food, motherhood, and spiritual resilience embodies the BMHW26 call. We gather at the Rock House not to study disparities, but to <strong>practice village</strong>. We cook. We witness. We eat. We build. This is what justice rooted in joy looks like.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- THE FOUR VOICES -->
<section class="section voices-section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2 class="section-title">The Four Voices</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Stories of food, birth, and village</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="voices-grid">
    <div class="voice-card">
      <div class="voice-name">KEISHA</div>
      <p class="voice-description">Woman of the mountains. Appalachian roots. Grandmother's kitchen. The weight of generations and the warmth of ancestral hands.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="voice-card">
      <div class="voice-name">ALTHEA</div>
      <p class="voice-description">Woman between worlds. Water births. Postpartum crisis. Ancestral visitation. The liminal space between drowning and rebirth.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="voice-card">
      <div class="voice-name">SIMONE</div>
      <p class="voice-description">Woman of liberation. Afro-Latinx traditions. Cumbia rhythms. Immigrant survival transformed into joy. Resistance in her hips, revolution in her recipes.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="voice-card">
      <div class="voice-name">MAYA</div>
      <p class="voice-description">Woman of joy reclaimed. Home birth in Charlottesville. Traditional midwife. Southern doula. Village present. Forty days of healing foods. Testimony that village WORKS.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

